<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The adaptive launcher icon, used from Android 8 onwards.

  Android 8 stopped drawing an app's icon as a fixed picture and started drawing it as two layers it
  masks itself - so the launcher decides whether Domix is a circle, a squircle or a rounded square,
  and every icon on the homescreen ends up the same shape. A single flat PNG still installs, but the
  system pillar-boxes it inside a white rounded square, which is why an app without this file looks
  like it was made before the phone was.

  background: flat brand orange, as a colour rather than a bitmap - it is one value, and a bitmap
  would be five files that can disagree.

  foreground: the white pin alone, on transparency, drawn at 560/1024 of the canvas. The size is the
  load-bearing part. The layer is 108dp but only the central 66dp is guaranteed to survive the mask,
  and launchers pan the rest during animations, so a glyph drawn to fill the canvas gets its edges
  cut off on exactly the devices nobody tests on. At this scale the pin's furthest point sits 280px
  from centre against the 313px the safe circle allows.

  monochrome: the same silhouette, which is what Android 13's themed icons tint to match the user's
  wallpaper. It works unchanged because the foreground is already a flat white shape rather than a
  picture - the tile gaps come through as gaps in the tinted glyph too.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
</adaptive-icon>
